window.GD=[
 {s:'how-to-choose-a-padel-racket',cat:'Buying guides',t:'How to choose a padel racket',m:'8 min',ph:'player choosing between rackets',d:'Shape, weight, core, face material and price all change how a racket plays. This guide shows you how to weigh them up and find a racket that suits the way you play now, not the way you hope to play next year.',sum:'Choose by level first, then shape, then weight and balance, and treat the core and face materials as fine tuning. For most new and improving players, a round or teardrop racket of around 355 to 370g with a soft or medium core is the safest place to start.',body:[
  ['p','A padel racket has no strings, just a foam core sealed inside a perforated face and a solid frame. That simple build hides a lot of variety. Two rackets that look almost identical on a shelf can play completely differently: one forgiving, comfortable and easy to swing, the other stiff, head heavy and only happy when it is hit hard and clean. Choosing well is less about finding the best racket on the market and more about finding the right one for you.'],
  ['p','We work through the decision in the order we would make it ourselves: your level, the shape, the weight and balance, then the materials. Get the first three right and you will be happy with almost any racket from a good brand. The materials are where you fine tune the feel.'],
  ['h','Start with your level'],
  ['p','Your level matters more than anything else on the spec sheet, because it decides how much help you need from the racket. A newer player hits the ball in lots of different places on the face, swings at different speeds from one shot to the next and has not yet built the timing to create pace on their own. They need a racket that forgives off-centre contact, gives the ball some free power at slower swing speeds and is kind to the arm.'],
  ['p','An improving player who plays once or twice a week, can hold a rally, volleys with some confidence and is starting to hit bandejas will want a little more control and a racket that rewards cleaner contact. Advanced players have consistent technique and fast swings, so they can handle a stiffer, more demanding racket and make use of the precision and power it gives back.'],
  ['p','Be honest with yourself here. The most common mistake we see is a newer player buying a stiff, head heavy racket because it is what the strongest players at the club use. It feels exciting on the odd big smash, but it punishes every mishit and makes the rest of the game harder. Every racket on our site is tagged by level, shape and weight, so you can filter to the band that fits you and compare like with like.'],
  ['shop','level:1'],
  ['h','Choose a shape'],
  ['p','Padel rackets come in three main shapes, and the shape largely decides where the sweet spot sits and how the weight is spread along the racket.'],
  ['p','Round rackets have the sweet spot low and central, close to the handle, and the weight sits towards your hand. That gives you control, a large and forgiving hitting area and a racket that is easy to move around at the net. They suit beginners, defensive players and anyone who values consistency over raw power, and plenty of experienced players stay with them for exactly those reasons.'],
  ['p','Diamond rackets are wide and angular at the top and narrow towards the throat, with the sweet spot high on the face and more weight in the head. They produce a lot of power on overheads and attacking volleys, but the sweet spot is smaller, the racket is slower to manoeuvre and it is less forgiving when you miss the centre. They are built for advanced, attacking players with sound technique.'],
  ['p','Teardrop rackets sit between the two, with a sweet spot a little higher than a round and a balance that is usually medium. They give a useful mix of control and power and suit a wide range of improving and all round players. If you are unsure, a teardrop is rarely a bad choice. Our guide to round, teardrop and diamond rackets goes into each shape in more detail.'],
  ['ph','three padel rackets laid side by side on court, one round, one teardrop and one diamond'],
  ['h','Get the weight and balance right'],
  ['p','Most adult padel rackets weigh somewhere between 340 and 385g. That range sounds narrow, but you will feel ten grams by the end of a long match. A lighter racket is quicker in fast exchanges at the net and easier on the shoulder. A heavier one is more stable when the ball arrives hard, carries more power through the ball and twists less when you hit away from the centre.'],
  ['p','As a starting point, most adult players are comfortable somewhere around 355 to 375g. If you are new to racket sports, have had shoulder or elbow trouble, or simply prefer a fast, reactive feel, look towards the lighter end. Stronger players who want stability and weight behind their overheads tend to move towards the top of the range.'],
  ['p','Balance tells you where that weight sits. A head heavy racket, also called high balance, feels more powerful on smashes and drives but is slower to bring around and puts more load on the forearm. A head light or low balance racket is easier to control and quicker in the hands. Balance is closely tied to shape, since round rackets are generally low balance and diamonds high, so once you have chosen a shape you are already most of the way there.'],
  ['tip','Listed weights are usually without an overgrip or protector tape, and each adds a few grams. An overgrip makes the head feel slightly lighter, while protector tape adds weight at the top of the frame.'],
  ['h','Understand the core'],
  ['p','Inside the frame sits the core, and it has a big effect on how the racket feels at impact. Most rackets use EVA rubber, a softer foam, or a combination of the two.'],
  ['p','EVA is a dense rubber that comes in different hardnesses. Hard EVA gives a firm, precise response and returns a lot of energy when the ball is struck hard, which is why advanced players like it. The trade off is that at slower swing speeds it can feel dead, and the ball does not come off the face as easily. Soft EVA is more forgiving: the ball sinks into the face a little more, comes off with less effort and the impact is gentler on the arm.'],
  ['p','Foam cores, often listed simply as foam or polyethylene foam, are softer still. They give plenty of ball exit at low speeds and feel very comfortable, which suits beginners and players who rely on touch. Foam tends to lose its liveliness sooner than EVA under heavy use, and it offers less control when you swing hard. Some rackets layer the two to get a little of each.'],
  ['p','As a rule of thumb, newer players and anyone with arm concerns should look for a soft or medium core. Players who hit hard and want precision will appreciate a harder core, provided they have the technique to make it work.'],
  ['h','Fibreglass or carbon'],
  ['p','The faces are laminated over the core, usually in fibreglass, carbon fibre or a mix of the two. Fibreglass is more flexible, so it gives the ball a little extra help at slow speeds, feels soft at impact and tends to keep the price down. It is a sound choice for beginners and many recreational players.'],
  ['p','Carbon is stiffer and lighter for its strength. A carbon face gives a firmer, more precise feel and rewards a fast, clean swing with power. It is also generally more durable than fibreglass. You will see carbon described as 3K, 12K or 18K. The number refers to how many thousand filaments are bundled into each strand of the weave. Different weaves change how the face flexes and feels, and brands use them to tune a racket, but a higher number is not automatically better. How the face, core and frame work together matters far more than the figure printed on the throat.'],
  ['p','Many rackets also have a rough or textured finish on the face, often described as sand or 3D texture. It helps the face grip the ball for slice and spin, which is useful on bandejas, viboras and serves. The texture wears smoother with use, which is worth knowing if spin is a big part of your game.'],
  ['q','Choosing well is less about finding the best racket on the market and more about finding the right one for you.'],
  ['h','Price, comfort and putting it together'],
  ['p','A more expensive racket is only a better racket for you if it matches your level. Top end models are usually stiffer and more specialised, built for players who swing fast and make clean contact. For a newer player, a well made beginner or intermediate racket will be more comfortable and more forgiving, and it will help you improve faster.'],
  ['p','Comfort deserves more attention than it usually gets. Padel involves a lot of short, sharp contact at the net, and a stiff, head heavy racket can aggravate the elbow and wrist if it is wrong for you. If you have had arm trouble, prioritise a soft core, a lower balance and a weight you can control comfortably, even if it means giving up a little power.'],
  ['p','Handle size is the last detail. Padel handles are fairly standard, and most players adjust the thickness with overgrips rather than looking for a different grip size. One overgrip over the base grip suits most hands, and a second adds thickness if the handle feels thin.'],
  ['ph','close up of a hand holding a padel racket handle wrapped with a fresh overgrip'],
  ['p','Put it all together and a few clear profiles emerge. A beginner, or anyone with a sore arm, will be happiest with a round racket at the lighter end of the range with a soft core and a fibreglass or soft carbon face. An improving player who is starting to attack will usually suit a round or teardrop with a medium core and a carbon face. An advanced player who spends most of the point at the net and likes to finish with overheads can look at a teardrop or diamond with a harder core and a stiffer face.'],
  ['p','Think about where you want to be in six months rather than five years. If you are playing every week and improving quickly, an intermediate racket gives you room to grow without making today harder. When your technique catches up, your racket can change with it. When it arrives, hold it, swing it at home and check the weight and balance feel right before you take it on court. Unused items can be returned within 14 days, so give yourself that moment to be sure.'],
  ['tip','If you are torn between two rackets, choose the more forgiving one. A racket that suits your good days and your bad ones will win you more points over a season.']
 ]},
 {s:'round-teardrop-or-diamond',cat:'Buying guides',t:'Round, teardrop or diamond?',m:'5 min',ph:'three racket shapes side by side',d:'The shape of a padel racket decides where the sweet spot sits and how its weight is spread. Here is how each shape plays and who it suits.',sum:'Round rackets give control and forgiveness, diamonds give power at the cost of a smaller sweet spot, and teardrops sit in between. Choose for your level and the way you play most points, not for the one shot you love.',body:[
  ['p','Look at a wall of padel rackets and the first thing you notice is the outline of the head. It is not just styling. The shape moves the sweet spot up or down the face, changes the balance and decides how much the racket helps you or asks of you. Almost every racket falls into one of three families: round, teardrop and diamond. Once you know how each one behaves, a shelf of rackets becomes much easier to read.'],
  ['h','Round: control and forgiveness'],
  ['p','A round racket has a circular head with the sweet spot low and in the middle of the face, closer to your hand. Because more of the weight sits towards the handle, round rackets usually have a low balance. They feel light and quick in the hand, even when the scales say they are no lighter than anything else, and they are easy to move into position for volleys and blocks.'],
  ['p','The big advantage is forgiveness. A low, central sweet spot is easier to find, and the face stays more consistent when you miss it. That makes round rackets the natural choice for beginners, but they are not only for beginners. Plenty of experienced players who defend from the back, rely on lobs and value placement over pace choose a round racket for exactly the same reasons. A round head is also the most comfortable option for players coming back from an elbow or shoulder problem, because the low balance asks less of the forearm on every shot.'],
  ['p','The trade off is power. With less weight in the head you have to supply more of the pace yourself on overheads. If you like to finish points with a smash, a round racket can feel as though it is holding you back.'],
  ['ph','three padel rackets side by side showing round, teardrop and diamond shapes'],
  ['h','Teardrop: the all rounder'],
  ['p','A teardrop racket is wider at the top than at the throat, with a sweet spot sitting a little higher than on a round. The balance is usually medium, so the head carries a bit more weight without feeling cumbersome. On court, that gives you more help on attacking shots while keeping a good amount of control and manoeuvrability.'],
  ['p','Teardrops suit the widest range of players. Improving players who have outgrown their first round racket often move to one, and many club players who switch between defending at the back and attacking at the net find a teardrop the most comfortable compromise. If you play on both sides of the court, or are not yet sure what kind of player you are becoming, this is a sensible place to start. For many club players a teardrop is the shape they stay with for years, because it adapts as their game develops.'],
  ['p','The category is broad. Some teardrops lean towards control, with a lower balance and a softer core, while others are close to a diamond in all but name. You will also see rackets described as hybrids, sitting between teardrop and round or between teardrop and diamond. Check the balance and core alongside the shape rather than assuming every teardrop plays the same.'],
  ['shop','shape:Teardrop'],
  ['h','Diamond: power, with conditions'],
  ['p','Diamond rackets have a wide, angular top that narrows towards the throat. The sweet spot is high on the face and the balance is high, with noticeably more weight in the head. When you connect cleanly on an overhead, the ball leaves the face fast, which is why attacking players love them for smashes and viboras.'],
  ['p','That power comes at a price. The sweet spot is smaller and further from your hand, so off-centre hits lose more pace and accuracy, and the extra weight in the head makes the racket slower in quick exchanges at the net. A head heavy racket also puts more strain on the forearm and elbow, particularly if your technique relies on the arm rather than turning the body.'],
  ['p','Diamonds are best for advanced players with consistent contact who spend most of the point at the net and are happy to take responsibility for finishing it. They are rarely the right first racket, and seldom the right second one.'],
  ['q','Choose the racket for the shots you play every rally, not the smash you hit once a set.'],
  ['h','Shape is only part of the picture'],
  ['p','Shape sets the character of a racket, but weight, core and face material decide how strongly that character comes through. A round racket with a hard core and a stiff carbon face can feel firm and demanding, while a diamond with a softer core and a lighter build can be more forgiving than its outline suggests. That is why two rackets of the same shape from different brands, or even two models from the same brand, can feel quite different on court.'],
  ['p','When you change shape, give yourself time to adjust. Moving from a round to a teardrop, you will notice the sweet spot sitting higher and the head feeling a little heavier, and your volleys may feel less precise for a session or two until your timing catches up. Moving to a diamond is a bigger step, and one worth taking only when your contact is consistent enough to find that higher sweet spot most of the time.'],
  ['h','Which shape is right for you'],
  ['p','Start with your level. Beginners and players with arm trouble should almost always begin with a round racket. Improving players are usually best served by a round racket or a control-minded teardrop. Advanced players can choose any shape, based on how they like to play.'],
  ['p','Then think about your role in the pair. Partners often settle into sides, and the sides tend to ask for different things. The player on the right usually builds the point, plays a lot of balls off the glass and needs consistency. The player on the left, whose forehand covers the middle of the court if they are right handed, often takes more of the overheads. A steady right side player will get more from a round racket or a control teardrop, while an attacking left side player with good technique may suit a power teardrop or a diamond.'],
  ['p','Finally, be wary of choosing for the one shot you love. A diamond might make your best smash better, but most points are decided by returns, volleys and lobs, and those are easier with a racket you control.'],
  ['tip','When you compare rackets on our site, filter by shape and level together. A round racket built for advanced players can be stiffer and more demanding than a teardrop made for improvers.']
 ]},
 {s:'racket-weight-and-balance-explained',cat:'Buying guides',t:'Racket weight and balance explained',m:'4 min',ph:'racket on scales',d:'Two rackets can weigh exactly the same and feel completely different. Here is what weight and balance really do, and how to choose the right numbers for you.',sum:'Most adult padel rackets weigh between 340 and 385g, and the balance decides whether that weight feels light or heavy in your hand. Choose a weight you can swing comfortably for a whole match, then use balance to tilt the racket towards control or power.',body:[
  ['p','Weight is the first number most players look at, and it matters. But on its own it only tells half the story. Where that weight sits along the racket, its balance, changes how heavy it feels to swing, how much power it gives you and how quickly you can react at the net. Understand both and a spec sheet becomes far easier to read.'],
  ['h','What weight does'],
  ['p','Most adult padel rackets weigh between 340 and 385g. A lighter racket is quicker to bring into position, easier to whip through the ball and less tiring on the shoulder over a long session. A heavier racket is more stable. It holds its line when a hard ball hits it, twists less on off-centre contact and carries more momentum into the ball, which you feel as power and solidity.'],
  ['p','Weight also helps in defence. When an opponent hits hard at you, a heavier racket absorbs and redirects the ball with less effort, while a very light one can be pushed around by the shot.'],
  ['p','Going too far either way causes problems. A racket that is too light can twist on mishits, sends more shock up the arm and encourages a wristy swing. One that is too heavy tires your shoulder and slows your reactions late in a match, which is often when strains creep in. As a starting point, most adult players are comfortable at around 355 to 375g. Newer players, juniors moving up to adult rackets and anyone with shoulder trouble usually do better at the lighter end, while strong players who want stability behind their overheads drift towards the top.'],
  ['ph','padel racket resting on a set of digital scales'],
  ['h','What balance does'],
  ['p','Balance describes where the weight is concentrated between the end of the handle and the tip of the head. Brands usually describe it as low, medium or high, and some quote a measurement in millimetres from the end of the handle, where a higher figure means the balance point sits further towards the head.'],
  ['p','A high balance, or head heavy racket, puts more mass where you strike the ball on overheads. It adds power to smashes and attacking volleys, but it is slower to bring around and asks more of your forearm. A low balance, or head light racket, keeps the mass closer to your hand. It feels quicker, more manoeuvrable and easier to control, especially in fast exchanges at the net and when you are digging out low balls.'],
  ['p','Medium balance sits in between, and it is where most teardrop rackets land. Shape and balance are closely linked, because the outline of the head moves both the mass and the sweet spot. Round rackets tend to be low balance and diamonds high.'],
  ['q','A head heavy racket can feel heavier to swing than a racket that weighs more on the scales.'],
  ['h','Why the two work together'],
  ['p','When you swing a racket, what you feel is not its weight on the scales but how hard it is to rotate around your wrist and shoulder. That depends on how far the mass sits from your hand. This is why a head heavy racket can feel more demanding than a heavier racket with a low balance, and why players moving from a round racket to a diamond often feel the new one is heavier when it is not.'],
  ['p','Compare the two figures together. If you want a more powerful feel without adding weight, a slightly higher balance will do it. If your current racket feels sluggish but you like its stability, look for a similar weight with a lower balance to get some speed back.'],
  ['shop','level:2'],
  ['h','Fine tuning'],
  ['p','Small changes add up. An overgrip adds a few grams at the handle, which lowers the balance slightly and makes the head feel lighter. Protector tape on the top of the frame does the opposite, adding a few grams at the head. Listed weights are normally without either, so expect your racket to weigh a little more once it is ready to play. Manufacturing tolerances also mean two rackets of the same model can differ by a few grams, which is normal and rarely noticeable on court.'],
  ['p','Some experienced players add thin strips of lead tape to the frame to raise the weight or move the balance point. It is a precise way to tune a racket you already know well, but make changes a little at a time, play a few sessions with each one, and only do it once you know exactly what you want to change.'],
  ['p','If you are caught between two weights, the lighter one is usually the safer choice for newer players. Good technique is easier to build with a racket you can swing freely, and a racket that feels slightly light in the first week rarely feels that way by the end of a long match.'],
  ['tip','If you have elbow or shoulder trouble, prioritise a low or medium balance over the lightest possible weight. A head light racket of moderate weight is usually kinder on the arm than a very light racket with a heavy head.'],
  ['p','Whatever you choose, give it a few sessions before you judge it. Your timing adapts to a new racket quickly, and the first hour rarely tells the whole story.']
 ]},
 {s:'choosing-padel-shoes',cat:'Footwear',t:'Choosing padel shoes',m:'5 min',ph:'shoe sole on court',d:'Padel is a game of short sprints, sudden stops and constant changes of direction on sand-filled artificial grass. The right shoes keep you stable, quick and on your feet.',sum:'Choose shoes made for padel or clay court tennis, with a sole that suits the courts you play on and firm support at the sides. Running shoes are built for straight lines and are the wrong tool for a sport that moves sideways.',body:[
  ['p','Of all the kit you buy for padel, shoes are the piece most often overlooked and the one most likely to cause problems if you get them wrong. Padel is played in a small space, with constant short sprints, lunges, stops and turns, mostly on artificial grass with sand worked into it. A shoe that grips too little lets you slip. One that grips unpredictably, or gives no support at the sides, puts your ankles and knees at risk.'],
  ['h','Why running shoes are not enough'],
  ['p','Running shoes are designed to move you forward in a straight line. They tend to have thick, soft cushioning, a raised heel and a sole pattern meant for roads and paths. On a padel court those features work against you. The soft, high midsole can roll over when you push off sideways, the upper offers little lateral support, and the sole either skates on the sand or grabs when you least expect it.'],
  ['p','A padel shoe sits lower to the ground, with a firmer, more stable platform and a reinforced upper that holds your foot in place when you change direction. It is built for the movement the sport actually asks of you.'],
  ['p','Tennis shoes are a closer match, but not all of them suit padel. Clay court tennis shoes use the same herringbone idea as most padel soles and work well. All court and hard court tennis shoes have flatter, smoother patterns designed to grip a hard surface, and on sand-filled turf they can skid when you need to stop.'],
  ['h','The sole: herringbone, omni and hybrid'],
  ['p','The outsole pattern is the biggest decision, and the right one depends on the courts you play on.'],
  ['p','A herringbone sole, the zigzag pattern borrowed from clay court tennis shoes, is the most common in padel. The grooves bite into sand-filled turf for grip while still letting the foot release as you turn, and they allow a small, controlled slide. It is a good all round choice and works well on most courts. Shoes labelled for clay are usually built on a full herringbone sole and suit padel well.'],
  ['p','An omni sole uses small rubber nubs or pimples across the outsole. These are designed for artificial grass with sand infill and give more grip, which helps on courts with a lot of loose sand or when the surface is damp. On newer courts with less sand, some players find them a little sticky when they turn.'],
  ['p','Many padel shoes now use a hybrid pattern, with herringbone in some zones and nubs in others, aiming for grip under the ball of the foot and a cleaner release at the heel and toe. If you play at several clubs with different courts, a herringbone or hybrid sole is the safest choice.'],
  ['ph','close up of the herringbone sole of a padel shoe on sand-filled artificial grass'],
  ['h','Support, cushioning and durability'],
  ['p','Look for firm support around the midfoot and heel, often with a stiffer or reinforced section along the outside of the shoe. That lateral support stops your foot rolling over the edge of the sole when you push off sideways, which is exactly when ankles go.'],
  ['p','Cushioning matters too, but more is not always better. You want enough under the heel to absorb the jumps and landings of overheads without the shoe becoming so soft or high that you lose stability. Padel shoes usually strike that balance with a lower, firmer midsole than a running shoe.'],
  ['p','Padel is hard on the front of a shoe, because you drag the toe of your back foot when serving and lunging for low balls. A reinforced toe cap or a wrap of tougher rubber at the front extends the life of the upper. A hard wearing outsole compound matters too, particularly if you play several times a week, as the abrasive sand on court wears soles down faster than most people expect.'],
  ['shop','cat:shoe'],
  ['h','Getting the fit right'],
  ['p','Fit varies between brands, so do not assume your size in one will be the same in another. Try shoes on in the afternoon or evening, when your feet are slightly larger, and wear the socks you play in.'],
  ['p','Your heel should sit snugly without lifting as you walk, and there should be roughly a thumb\'s width between your longest toe and the end of the shoe. The forefoot should feel secure but not cramped, because your feet spread as you land and push off. Some models run narrow and others are cut wider, which is worth checking if you have broad feet.'],
  ['p','Lace them properly every time. Padel puts a lot of sideways load through the shoe, and a loosely laced foot slides inside the upper however good the support is. If your heel lifts, using the top eyelet to lock the laces around the ankle usually fixes it. Good sports socks with a little cushioning under the heel and forefoot help too, and they reduce blisters on the abrasive surface.'],
  ['p','Most padel shoes need little breaking in, but it still makes sense to wear a new pair for a shorter session or two before a long match, so any pressure points show up early.'],
  ['tip','Try new shoes on indoors on a clean floor first. Unused items can be returned within 14 days, so check the fit properly before they touch a court.'],
  ['h','Wet courts and when to replace'],
  ['p','Many UK courts are outdoors, or covered but open at the sides, so you will often play on a damp surface. Wet artificial grass is slippery, and that is where a grippy sole earns its place. If most of your padel is played outdoors through the autumn and winter, lean towards an omni or hybrid sole.'],
  ['p','Check your soles regularly. When the pattern under the ball of your foot has worn smooth, grip drops sharply, even if the upper still looks fine. Worn soles are a common cause of slips, so replace your shoes once the tread has gone in the areas you push off from, and do not keep an old pair going just because it is comfortable.'],
  ['p','Look after them between sessions too. Knock the sand out after playing, let wet shoes dry naturally away from radiators, and keep them for the court rather than wearing them to and from the club, which wears the sole down without doing your padel any good.'],
  ['q','When the tread under the ball of your foot has worn smooth, the shoe has done its job, however good the upper still looks.']
 ]},
 {s:'padel-rules-made-simple',cat:'Beginners',t:'Padel rules made simple',m:'3 min',ph:'court from behind the glass',d:'Everything you need to know to play your first proper game, from the serve to the walls.',sum:'Serve underarm and diagonally after one bounce, then play every ball on the volley or after a single bounce. The walls on your side are part of the game, but your shot must land on the floor before it touches any wall on the other side.',body:[
  ['p','Padel is almost always played as doubles, on an enclosed court with glass walls at each end and side walls made partly of glass and partly of wire mesh. The scoring comes from tennis and the serve is simpler, but the walls change everything. Here are the rules you need for your first game.'],
  ['h','The serve'],
  ['p','Every point starts with an underarm serve. The server stands behind the service line, bounces the ball once on the ground behind that line and strikes it at or below waist height. At least one foot must be on the ground at the moment of contact, and you cannot walk, run or jump into the serve.'],
  ['p','The ball must travel diagonally over the net and bounce in the opponent\'s service box. After it bounces it may hit the glass, but if it touches the wire mesh before the receiver plays it, it is a fault. As in tennis you get two attempts. A serve that clips the net and lands in the correct box is a let and is taken again, unless it then hits the mesh, which makes it a fault. The receiver must let the serve bounce before playing it.'],
  ['p','The first point of every game is served from the right hand side, then the server switches sides after each point. In the receiving pair, one player takes every serve on the right and the other every serve on the left, and they keep those sides for the whole set.'],
  ['h','Playing the point'],
  ['p','Once the serve has been returned, you can volley the ball or let it bounce once on your side. Volleying is allowed at any time except on the return of serve. After that bounce it can hit any wall on your side, glass or mesh, and you can still play it as long as it has not bounced on the ground a second time.'],
  ['p','When you return the ball, it must cross the net and land on the floor of your opponents\' side before it touches any of their walls. If it hits their glass or mesh first, it is out. You are allowed to play the ball into your own walls to get it back over the net, which is a handy escape when a ball gets past you.'],
  ['ph','view from behind the back glass of a doubles padel match in play'],
  ['h','How you lose a point'],
  ['p','You lose the point if the ball bounces twice on your side, if your shot goes into the net, or if it hits the other side\'s walls or fence, or leaves the court, before bouncing on their floor. You also lose it if the ball touches you or your partner, if either of you touches the net while the ball is in play, or if you hit the ball twice. A ball that bounces on your side and then leaves the court is your opponents\' point, unless the court has open exits and you have agreed to allow play outside.'],
  ['h','Scoring and changing ends'],
  ['p','Scoring follows tennis: 15, 30, 40 and game, with sets of six games and a tie break at six all. At 40 all you play either advantage or golden point, so agree which before you start. Pairs change ends after the first game of each set and then after every two games.'],
  ['h','The ball and your racket'],
  ['p','Padel balls look like tennis balls but have slightly lower pressure, so they bounce a little less, which suits the enclosed court. A fresh tin makes a real difference to how the walls play. Rackets have no strings, and the cord on the handle must be worn around your wrist while you play.'],
  ['shop','cat:ball'],
  ['tip','Before the first serve, agree the points that vary between clubs, such as golden point and whether play outside the court is allowed. It saves arguments later.']
 ]},
 {s:'your-first-padel-lesson',cat:'Beginners',t:'Your first padel lesson',m:'4 min',ph:'coach with beginners',d:'What to expect, what to bring and what you will learn in your first hour on court.',sum:'Wear proper court shoes and comfortable sportswear, and expect to cover the grip, the ready position, simple strokes, volleys and the walls. Keep your swings short, talk to your partner and let the glass give you time.',body:[
  ['p','Padel is one of the easiest racket sports to pick up, and a first lesson is the quickest way through the awkward stage. A good coach will have you rallying within the hour, explain how the walls work and teach the habits that make every game afterwards more fun.'],
  ['h','Booking and what to bring'],
  ['p','Most clubs offer group lessons for beginners as well as private sessions. Four is the natural group size for padel, because you learn the game as it is played, in pairs. A private lesson gives you more individual attention, while a group session with three friends is a sociable way to start and gives you a ready made four for your first games. If you have played tennis or squash, tell the coach. They will adjust the session, and tennis players in particular tend to swing too big at first.'],
  ['p','Wear comfortable sportswear you can move freely in. Shoes matter more than anything else. Padel courts are artificial grass with sand, and running shoes are not built for constant sideways movement on that surface. Padel shoes, or clay court tennis shoes with a herringbone sole, are ideal. Many clubs have rackets to hire and provide balls for lessons, so you do not need your own racket to begin. Bring water and arrive ten minutes early so you are warmed up when the lesson starts.'],
  ['shop','cat:shoe'],
  ['h','What you will learn'],
  ['p','A typical beginner lesson starts with a short warm up, moves on to drills where the coach feeds you ball after ball to practise one shot at a time, and often finishes with a short game so you can put it all together. Do not worry if that game feels chaotic. Everyone\'s first games do.'],
  ['p','Most lessons begin with the continental grip, holding the handle roughly as you would hold a hammer, with the racket face on its edge. It works for forehands, backhands, volleys and serves, so you do not have to change grip during fast exchanges.'],
  ['p','Next comes the ready position: knees soft, weight forward and the racket held up in front of you. From there a coach will usually feed you simple forehands and backhands from the back of the court. Padel swings are compact. The ball is slower and the court smaller than in tennis, so a short backswing and a controlled follow through give far more consistency than a big swing.'],
  ['p','You will then move up to the net to learn volleys, which are short blocks rather than swings, and the serve, which is hit underarm after a bounce and is much easier to learn than a tennis serve.'],
  ['ph','coach feeding balls to a group of four beginners at the net'],
  ['h','Meeting the walls'],
  ['p','The walls are what make padel different, and most first lessons introduce them early. The key idea is that you do not have to hit the ball before it reaches the back glass. Let it bounce, let it come off the wall, turn with it and play it once it has slowed down and dropped in front of you. It feels strange at first, because instinct tells you to chase the ball. Within a few attempts most beginners discover that the wall buys them time. The side glass works in the same way, although the ball comes off at an angle, so move your feet early and give yourself room.'],
  ['q','The wall is on your side. Let the ball come off it and you will have more time than you think.'],
  ['p','Your coach will also show you the lob, the shot that sends the ball high over the opponents at the net and pushes them back. It is one of the most important shots in padel, and one beginners can use straight away.'],
  ['h','Habits worth building early'],
  ['p','Talk to your partner. Calling mine or yours on balls down the middle avoids clashes, and moving together as a pair, forward to the net and back again, is central to the game. When one of you goes up, the other follows. If you are not sure where to aim, go through the middle. A ball between the two opponents causes hesitation, gives them less angle to work with and is the safest target when you are under pressure.'],
  ['p','Be patient with power. Hitting hard from the back of the court is the fastest way to lose points, because a fast ball rebounds off the glass and hands your opponents a comfortable shot. Placement, height and consistency win far more points at every level.'],
  ['tip','Watch the ball all the way onto the racket, especially when it comes off the glass. Most beginner mistakes come from looking up at the target too early.'],
  ['p','One lesson gets you playing, but a few in a row make the basics stick. Many clubs also run social sessions for newer players, which are a good way to find partners at your level. When you start playing every week, it is worth buying your own racket. A round racket with a soft core and a moderate weight is the usual first choice, and every racket on our site is tagged by level, so you can find one built for beginners.']
 ]},
 {s:'how-padel-scoring-works',cat:'Beginners',t:'How padel scoring works',m:'3 min',ph:'scoreboard at a club',d:'Padel uses tennis scoring with a couple of twists. Here is how points, games, sets and tie breaks work, and the deuce options you will meet at clubs.',sum:'Points go 15, 30, 40 and game, a set is the first to six games with a two game lead, and a tie break settles the set at six all. At 40 all you play either advantage or a single golden point, so agree which before you start.',body:[
  ['p','If you have watched tennis, you already know most of padel scoring. If you have not, it takes a game or two to get used to and then becomes second nature. Here is how it all fits together.'],
  ['h','Points and games'],
  ['p','A game is won by the first pair to win four points, but the points are called 15, 30, 40 and game rather than one, two, three and four. Zero is called love. The serving pair\'s score is said first, so 30 to 15 means the servers have won two points and the receivers one.'],
  ['p','One player serves every point of a game, starting from the right hand side and switching sides after each point. The serve then passes to the other pair, and the partners within each pair take turns, so all four players serve in rotation through the set. At the start of each set, each pair decides which partner serves first. The receiving pair also decides who receives on the right and who on the left, and they keep those sides until the set is over.'],
  ['h','Deuce, advantage and golden point'],
  ['p','When both pairs reach 40, the score is deuce, or 40 all, and there are two ways to settle it. With advantage scoring, the pair that wins the next point has the advantage. Win the following point too and they take the game. Lose it and the score goes back to deuce, which can carry on for some time.'],
  ['p','With golden point, the next point after deuce decides the game, and the receiving pair chooses which of them receives it. Golden point keeps matches moving and adds a bit of pressure, which is why many leagues and social sessions use it. Some professional events use a variation that allows a limited number of advantages before a deciding point.'],
  ['p','Golden point changes the tactics as well as the length of the match. Receivers usually pick whichever of them returns better from that side, and servers tend to favour their most reliable serve rather than taking risks. Treat it like a tie break of one point: keep it simple, keep the ball in play and make your opponents earn it.'],
  ['q','Agree on golden point or advantage before the first ball, not at 40 all.'],
  ['h','Sets and tie breaks'],
  ['p','A set is won by the first pair to reach six games with a two game lead, so 6 to 4 wins a set but 6 to 5 does not. At 6 to 5 you play another game. If the leaders win it, they take the set 7 to 5. If not, it goes to a tie break at six all.'],
  ['p','In the tie break, points are counted one, two, three and so on, and the first pair to seven with a two point lead wins the set 7 to 6. The player due to serve takes the first point, then each player serves two points in turn, and pairs change ends after every six points.'],
  ['p','Matches are usually the best of three sets. Some leagues and social formats replace the third set with a single tie break, first to ten points with a two point lead, which keeps the match within a court booking.'],
  ['ph','club scoreboard beside a padel court showing a set score'],
  ['h','Changing ends'],
  ['p','Pairs swap ends after the first game of each set and then after every two games, which means after the odd numbered games. It keeps things fair when one end has more sun, wind or glare.'],
  ['tip','The server should call the score clearly before every point. It saves arguments and keeps everyone in rhythm.'],
  ['p','Once the scoring makes sense, the best way to learn the rest is to play. If you are still borrowing a racket, a beginner racket with a round head and a soft core will make your first matches easier.'],
  ['shop','level:1']
 ]},
 {s:'the-bandeja-step-by-step',cat:'Technique',t:'The bandeja, step by step',m:'4 min',ph:'player hitting a bandeja',d:'The bandeja is the shot that keeps you at the net when a lob goes over your head. Here is how to hit it, where to aim it and how it differs from the vibora and the smash.',sum:'The bandeja is a controlled, sliced overhead that keeps you at the net rather than trying to win the point outright. Turn early, keep the racket face slightly open, make contact in front of you at around head height and send the ball deep so it stays low off the glass.',body:[
  ['p','Every pair that takes the net will be lobbed. You can run back, let the ball bounce and give up your position, or you can take it in the air with a bandeja and stay where the points are won. That is why the bandeja is one of the most important shots in padel, and usually the first overhead a coach will teach.'],
  ['h','What the bandeja is for'],
  ['p','Bandeja means tray in Spanish, after the way the racket is held up and flat, like a waiter carrying a tray. Its job is not to win the point. It is a defensive overhead played from an attacking position: a controlled, sliced shot that sends the ball deep, keeps your opponents at the back and gives you time to hold the net.'],
  ['p','That sets it apart from the smash, which is hit hard, flat or with topspin to finish the point, and from the vibora, a more aggressive overhead hit with more side spin and pace. The vibora is a useful weapon once your bandeja is reliable, but the bandeja comes first. The vibora is usually struck further to the side of the body, with more wrist and a more side-on cut, so it skids low off the side glass. That makes it harder for opponents to deal with, but also harder to control.'],
  ['p','Played well, the bandeja also keeps steady pressure on your opponents. They have to deal with a deep, low ball from the back of the court while you and your partner wait at the net for their reply, which is exactly where you want them.'],
  ['h','Setting up'],
  ['p','Preparation decides everything. As soon as you see the lob, turn sideways so that your non-hitting shoulder points towards the net, and move back with side steps or crossover steps rather than backpedalling. Use small adjusting steps at the end so the ball will drop slightly in front of you and to the side of your hitting shoulder.'],
  ['p','Hold the racket in a continental grip. Lift it early, with your elbow at around shoulder height and the racket head above your head, the face slightly open. Point your free hand at the ball. It helps your balance and timing, and it keeps your shoulders turned until the moment you swing.'],
  ['ph','player at the net turned sideways preparing a bandeja with the racket raised and free hand pointing at the ball'],
  ['h','Making contact'],
  ['p','Let the ball drop a little. Hit it in front of your body at around head height, rather than at full stretch above you. Swing forward and across the ball, slicing it with the open face so the racket travels from high to low and slightly across your body. The action is smooth rather than violent. Most of the control comes from the slice and the follow through, not from arm speed.'],
  ['p','Finish with the racket moving towards your target and coming across in front of you, then move forward towards the net straight away. A bandeja followed by a step backwards gives away everything it was meant to win.'],
  ['q','The bandeja is not meant to win the point. It is meant to keep you at the net so you can win the next one.'],
  ['h','Where to aim'],
  ['p','The classic target is deep and cross court, so the ball lands near the back glass and comes off it low, ideally towards the corner where the opponent has to deal with the side wall too. A deep bandeja down the middle also works well, especially against a pair who are not sure who should take it.'],
  ['p','Keep it low over the net and deep. A bandeja that lands short and sits up is an invitation for your opponents to attack, and one hit too hard will come off the back glass at a comfortable height for them. Depth and a low bounce matter far more than pace.'],
  ['tip','If you cannot reach the lob comfortably in front of you, do not force a bandeja. Let it bounce, play it off the glass and win the net back later in the point.'],
  ['h','Common mistakes'],
  ['p','The most common fault is turning it into a smash, swinging hard and flat and watching the ball fly off the back glass into the opponents\' strike zone. Close behind come turning too late, letting the ball drift behind your head and flicking it with the wrist instead of using the shoulder and the slice. Each one costs control. Finally, many players hit a good bandeja and then admire it from the service line. Recover to the net every time, because that position is the reason you played the shot.'],
  ['h','Practising it'],
  ['p','Start close to the net with a partner feeding you gentle, short lobs. Concentrate on the early turn, the raised racket and a contact point in front of you, and do not worry about where the ball lands yet. Once the action feels natural, ask for deeper lobs so you have to move back before you hit, then add a target near the back glass on the opposite side. A cone or a towel in the corner gives you something specific to aim at and shows you quickly whether your depth is consistent.'],
  ['p','Your racket plays a part too. A round or teardrop racket with a low or medium balance is easier to control on a sliced overhead than a head heavy diamond, which makes the bandeja simpler to groove as you improve.'],
  ['shop','level:2']
 ]},
 {s:'getting-the-most-from-the-glass',cat:'Technique',t:'Getting the most from the glass',m:'4 min',ph:'player playing off the back wall',d:'The walls are what make padel padel. Learn to read the back and side glass and you will have more time, fewer panicked shots and better options in defence.',sum:'Let the ball come off the glass, turn with it and play it as it drops in front of you. Use the back wall to buy time, lob from defence, and keep the wire mesh out of your plans.',body:[
  ['p','New players treat the walls as a problem. Experienced players treat them as a partner. Almost every ball that passes you at the back of the court can still be played after it comes off the glass, and once you trust that, the game slows down and your choices improve.'],
  ['h','Why letting the ball go helps'],
  ['p','When a fast ball comes at you deep in the court, hitting it early means taking it on the rise or at an awkward height, often off balance. If you let it bounce and come off the back glass, it loses pace and drops into a more comfortable zone. You get extra time, a better contact point and the chance to choose your shot rather than just react.'],
  ['p','The trick is trusting the rebound. Glass gives a consistent bounce, so a ball that arrives fast and low will come off quickly and low, while a slower, higher ball comes off gently and higher. The more you watch the wall, the better you become at predicting where the ball will end up.'],
  ['q','The glass gives you time. All you have to do is let it.'],
  ['h','Reading the back wall'],
  ['p','Do not stand with your back against the glass. Give yourself a stride or two of room so the ball has space to come out to you. As the ball passes, turn with it, opening your body towards the side wall, and keep your eyes on it as it hits the glass. Then move forward with the ball as it comes off, so you strike it slightly in front of you as it drops, rather than reaching back for it.'],
  ['p','Keep your swing short and the racket face slightly open. Most balls off the back wall are best played with a lob or with a soft, low ball that dips at the feet of the opponents at the net. Both are far more effective than trying to drive through them.'],
  ['p','Where the ball bounces tells you a lot. A ball that lands close to the glass meets the wall low and tends to come off sharply and stay near it, so you may need to stay closer and play it quickly. One that lands further from the glass climbs higher up the wall and comes further out into the court, giving you more time. Your partner can help here: a clear call to leave a ball that is going to come off the glass saves you from a rushed shot.'],
  ['ph','player turned towards the back glass, waiting for the ball to come off the wall'],
  ['h','Corners and side glass'],
  ['p','A ball that hits the side glass and then the back glass, or the other way round, changes direction twice and comes back out roughly along the line it went in, but slower and later than you expect. Give corner balls even more room than a straight back wall ball, wait for the second rebound and play them late.'],
  ['p','A ball off the side glass alone travels across the court and tends to stay close to the wall. Move your feet so there is space between you and the glass when you hit, and keep your swing compact so the racket does not clip the wall.'],
  ['p','The wire mesh is a different story. A ball that bounces into the mesh comes off unpredictably, often dead or at a strange angle. Get to it if you can, but never build your plan around it.'],
  ['h','Choosing your shot off the glass'],
  ['p','From the back of the court, the ball you play off the glass is usually a defensive one. The lob is your best friend: a high, deep ball over the net players forces them back and lets you and your partner take the net. A low, soft ball at their feet as they come in is the other good option.'],
  ['p','When the ball comes off the back glass high and slowly, you can attack it. Stepping in and hitting down on it with a controlled, sliced drive, sometimes called a bajada, turns defence into attack. Pick these moments carefully. Most balls off the glass reward patience, not power.'],
  ['p','When you and your partner are both at the back, stay roughly level and leave each other room. If one of you is drawn into the corner, the other shifts slightly towards the middle, ready for the next ball. Defending as a pair, with both of you patient off the glass, is how you wait for the short ball that lets you attack.'],
  ['p','Control matters more than pace in all of this, and a round racket with a low balance makes the small, late adjustments of glass play easier.'],
  ['shop','shape:Round'],
  ['h','Using your own wall as an escape'],
  ['p','When the ball has got past you and is about to bounce a second time, you can hit it into your own back wall so it rebounds up and over the net. It is a last resort, but it is legal and better than giving up the point. Hit upwards and firmly into the glass so the ball travels high over the net, ideally deep enough to act like a lob.'],
  ['tip','Ask a partner to feed balls deep into your back glass for ten minutes at the start of a session. Repetition builds trust in the rebound faster than anything else.']
 ]},
 {s:'serving-well-in-padel',cat:'Technique',t:'Serving well in padel',m:'3 min',ph:'player serving',d:'The padel serve is not about aces. It is about starting the point on your terms and getting to the net.',sum:'Bounce the ball behind the line, strike it at or below waist height and aim for consistency, depth and placement over power. Then follow your serve to the net, because that is where the serving pair has the advantage.',body:[
  ['p','Because the serve is underarm and struck no higher than the waist, it will rarely win a point outright. What it can do is set the point up. A well placed serve gives the receiver an awkward ball and gives you the time to move forward to the net, where most padel points are won.'],
  ['h','The rules in brief'],
  ['p','Stand behind the service line, bounce the ball behind it and strike it at or below waist height with at least one foot on the ground. The ball must go diagonally over the net and bounce in the opponent\'s service box. It may hit the glass after bouncing, but if it touches the wire mesh it is a fault. You have two attempts, and a serve that clips the net and lands in the box is replayed.'],
  ['h','A reliable action'],
  ['p','Use a continental grip and stand side on, with your front shoulder pointing roughly towards the target. Drop the ball slightly in front of you and to the side, let it bounce, and strike it on the way down at about hip height, comfortably below the waist. Swing smoothly forward and through, keeping the racket face steady. A consistent ball drop gives you a consistent serve, so practise the bounce as much as the swing.'],
  ['ph','player serving underarm with the ball at hip height and the racket face slightly open'],
  ['h','Where to aim'],
  ['p','There are three main targets. Serving towards the side glass makes the ball bounce in the box and then come off the side wall, which forces the receiver to adjust and is especially awkward on their backhand. Serving down the middle, towards where the centre line meets the service line, narrows the receiver\'s angles and makes a wide return harder. Serving at the body cramps the receiver and often draws a weak return.'],
  ['p','Depth matters as much as direction. A serve that lands deep, near the service line, pushes the receiver back. A short serve invites them to step in and attack. Mix your targets and spins through a match so the receiver can never settle into a rhythm, and save the serve they have struggled with for the points that matter.'],
  ['h','Spin, pace and percentages'],
  ['p','A little slice makes the ball skid and stay low after the bounce and after it hits the glass, which is hard to return well. Topspin makes it kick up higher. Pace is less useful than it looks, because a fast serve comes back to you just as fast and leaves you less time to reach the net.'],
  ['p','Aim to make your first serve almost every time. A safe second serve gives away the initiative, and a double fault gives away the point. Your second serve should use the same action as your first, with a little more height over the net and a target further inside the box, rather than a slow, pushed ball that the receiver can attack.'],
  ['q','The serve does not need to win the point. It needs to get you to the net.'],
  ['h','Getting to the net'],
  ['p','After serving, move forward with a few quick steps and land in a balanced split step as the receiver strikes the ball. Your partner is already at the net, so arriving together lets you take control of the point. Watch for the lob return, which is the receiver\'s best answer to a good serve.'],
  ['p','While you serve, your partner stands at the net in the opposite service box, a couple of metres from the net and close to the middle of the box. From there they can cut off a straight return, cover part of the middle and still turn quickly if the receiver lobs over them.'],
  ['tip','Practise on your own with a handful of balls, aiming at a target in each corner of the service box. Ten minutes of this before a match does more for your serve than any amount of hitting hard.'],
  ['shop','cat:ball']
 ]},
 {s:'make-your-racket-last',cat:'Kit care',t:'Make your racket last',m:'3 min',ph:'racket with protector tape',d:'A good padel racket is an investment. A few simple habits keep it playing well for longer and help it survive the odd clash with the floor.',sum:'Protect the frame with tape, keep the racket away from extreme heat and cold, and store it in a cover or a thermal bag. Clean the face gently and check it regularly for cracks.',body:[
  ['p','Padel rackets are tougher than they look, but they are not indestructible. The core, the laminated faces and the frame can all be damaged by knocks, temperature and careless storage. Most of that damage is avoidable, and the habits that prevent it take seconds.'],
  ['h','Protect the frame'],
  ['p','The top of the frame takes most of the punishment. It scrapes the floor when you scoop up low balls, catches the walls when you play close to the glass and meets your partner\'s racket in the middle of the court. Protector tape is a thin, tough film that wraps around the top of the frame and takes those knocks instead of the racket. It is inexpensive, easy to replace and worth fitting from day one.'],
  ['p','Replace the tape when it starts to peel or wears through. It adds a few grams at the head, so you may notice a slightly higher balance at first, but you soon adjust.'],
  ['ph','padel racket frame with clear protector tape fitted around the top'],
  ['shop','cat:acc'],
  ['h','Heat, cold and storage'],
  ['p','Temperature is the hidden enemy. A racket left in a hot car can soften the core and the resins in the face, which affects how it plays and how well it holds together. Cold has the opposite effect. A racket left in a freezing car overnight becomes stiffer and more brittle, and a hard ball early in a winter session can crack a face that would have survived in normal conditions.'],
  ['p','Store your racket indoors at room temperature, and carry it in a cover or a racket bag with a thermal compartment when you travel to play. Keep it out of the car boot between sessions and away from radiators, and never leave it under a pile of heavy kit. On a hot day, keep it in the shade or in your bag between matches rather than lying in full sun by the court.'],
  ['q','Most racket damage happens off the court, in a car boot or a crowded kit bag, not in the middle of a rally.'],
  ['tip','In cold weather, let your racket warm up gradually with a few minutes of gentle hitting before you play hard.'],
  ['h','Cleaning and checking'],
  ['p','Wipe the face with a slightly damp cloth after playing to remove sand and dust. Avoid solvents, cleaning sprays and abrasive pads, which can damage the finish and wear away a textured surface. Let the racket dry naturally before putting it away.'],
  ['p','Check regularly for cracks in the frame and face, particularly around the throat and the top of the frame. A small chip in the paint is usually cosmetic, but a crack you can feel, or a racket that starts to rattle or sound different, needs a closer look.'],
  ['h','Habits on court'],
  ['p','Never hit the floor or the wall with your racket in frustration, and avoid scooping balls off the ground with the frame. Call for balls down the middle so you and your partner do not clash, and always wear the wrist cord, which is part of the rules and stops a racket flying into the glass.'],
  ['p','Look after the handle as well as the head. A worn, slippery overgrip makes you grip harder and makes it more likely the racket slips out of your hand, so replace it regularly. If the base grip underneath starts to split or lose its cushioning, replace it before it affects your hold on the racket.'],
  ['h','Knowing when it is finished'],
  ['p','Even with perfect care, a racket played several times a week slowly loses some liveliness as the core ages. The signs are gradual: the ball comes off the face with less pace than it used to, the sound at impact turns dull, or the face feels softer and less precise. A racket with a structural crack in the frame or face should be retired, because it will only get worse. Good habits will not stop that process, but they keep your racket at its best for as long as possible.']
 ]},
 {s:'when-to-change-your-overgrip',cat:'Kit care',t:'When to change your overgrip',m:'2 min',ph:'overgrip being wrapped',d:'A worn overgrip costs you control and puts extra strain on your arm. Here is how to tell when it is time for a new one, and how to fit it.',sum:'Change your overgrip when it turns shiny, slippery, hard or torn, which for frequent players can mean every week or two. Fitting a new one takes a couple of minutes.',body:[
  ['p','The overgrip is the thin tape wrapped over your racket handle. It gives you grip, absorbs sweat and lets you adjust the thickness of the handle. It is also the cheapest part of your kit and the one players most often forget to replace.'],
  ['h','Signs it is time'],
  ['p','A fresh overgrip feels soft and slightly tacky. When it turns smooth, shiny or hard, slips in your hand as you sweat, or starts to darken, tear or unravel, it is time for a new one. If you find yourself squeezing harder to stop the racket turning in your hand, the grip has already gone.'],
  ['p','How often depends on how much you play and how much you sweat. Someone on court several times a week may need a new overgrip every week or two, while an occasional player might go a month or more. Change it before a match that matters, not after one you lost.'],
  ['h','Why it matters'],
  ['p','A slippery grip makes you hold the racket tighter, and a tight grip tenses the forearm and loads the elbow. A fresh overgrip lets you hold the racket loosely, which improves feel and touch on volleys and keeps your arm relaxed through a long session. It also keeps the racket where you put it. A handle that turns slightly at impact sends the ball somewhere you did not intend, and on a fast exchange at the net that is often the point gone.'],
  ['h','Fitting a new one'],
  ['p','Unwrap the old overgrip and remove any tape. Start at the butt of the handle with the tapered end, and wrap upwards in the same direction as the base grip, keeping steady tension and overlapping each turn slightly. Finish just below the throat, trim if needed and secure the end with the finishing tape.'],
  ['ph','hands wrapping a white overgrip onto a padel racket handle'],
  ['tip','If the handle feels thin, add a second overgrip rather than wrapping the first one tighter.'],
  ['h','Choosing the right one'],
  ['p','Most overgrips are either tacky or dry. Tacky overgrips have a slightly sticky feel that holds the hand well and suits most players. Dry overgrips feel more like a towel and absorb sweat, which helps if your hands get very wet, particularly indoors or in warm weather. If your racket keeps slipping despite a fresh tacky grip, try a dry one.'],
  ['p','The overgrip is not the same as the base grip, the thicker, cushioned grip fitted to the handle at the factory. The base grip lasts much longer and only needs replacing when it has compressed, split or lost its cushioning. Keep a spare overgrip in your bag, because they always wear out at the least convenient moment.'],
  ['shop','cat:acc']
 ]}
];
